"## Scope\n\nProve externally performed multi-commit squash delivery without weakening exact-head delivery authority.\n\nWhen a pull request is merged outside the governed `deliver` provider action — for example squash-merged by a maintainer in the GitHub UI — the delivery verifier must still be able to prove, from evidence alone, that the merge was a squash of the accepted head. Today it can only do so when the pull request contained exactly one non-merge source commit (`provesSingleSourceSquash`, `scripts/task-tracker/lib/delivery-verification.mjs`). Every multi-commit externally squash-merged pull request reaches `delivery-verification:merge-method-unknown` and can never obtain a receipt, so its issue can never close.\n\nGitHub supplies no authoritative historical merge method. Verified live against PR #1489: the REST payload exposes `merged`, `merged_by`, `merge_commit_sha`, `mergeable`, `mergeable_state`, and `auto_merge`, none of which records the method used; `rebaseable` and `squash_merge_commit_title` are both null after merge. The production adapter correspondingly never populates `pullRequest.mergeMethod`, so `mergeMethodObservation` is always null on this path. A complete topology proof is therefore required rather than preferred.\n\nIn scope: a multi-source squash proof at the evidence-verification boundary that binds the complete pull-request source inventory to the accepted head, proves the merged tree is exactly the accepted tree, and fails closed on any incomplete or ambiguous evidence.\n\nOut of scope for this issue: general post-approval head-advance recovery. The earlier framing of this issue claimed such an advance \"permanently orphans\" delivery. That was wrong on both counts — the immediate blocker is the multi-commit squash proof, and re-verification demonstrably realigns the accepted SHA (done live on #1488, realigned to 82eba885). No head-advance recovery will be added unless a separate failing test proves it is required to close #1485 or #1488.\n\n"